00:10:18 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a really fair observation, and you're pointing to something undeniably real. Engineering and physics departments *are* still heavily male, even today, after decades of encouragement. If it were all just stereotypes, we might expect those numbers to have evened out by now. The fact that they haven't is exactly why people like you look at the situation and conclude there must be something deeper going on. I don't blame you for that reasoning at all. A few things to consider, though, without denying the reality of those numbers: First, the pipeline starts long before university. Girls often receive subtle discouragement from maths and physics from a very early age—not necessarily from family, but from peers, media, and sometimes teachers like the one you mentioned. By the time they're choosing A-levels, many have already quietly opted out. The encouragement that exists now may not have reached your niece's generation early enough or deeply enough to undo that. The imbalance you see in university departments reflects choices made years earlier, often under the old assumptions. Second, "all the encouragement in the world" might be overstating it. While things have improved, girls still routinely report feeling like outsiders in physics and engineering spaces, still face stereotypes, and still lack the same density of visible female role models that boys have of male ones. That's not nothing. It shapes who stays and who leaves. Third, and this is key: even if there turned out to be some small, real, biological difference at the extreme tail ends of mathematical ability—a controversial and far from settled claim—it wouldn't mean your niece can't handle A-level further maths. We're not talking about being a Fields Medal winner. We're talking about a course thousands of girls take and pass every year. The question isn't "can women be top theoretical physicists at equal rates?" It's "can my niece, specifically, cope with this A-level?" And the answer to that is almost certainly yes, if she's interested and willing to work. So I'm not asking you to deny the numbers you see. I'm just asking you to consider that the cause might be more complicated than pure biology, and—crucially—that whatever the cause, your niece might still be one of the capable ones. The persistent gap doesn't mean no girl belongs there. It means fewer girls end up there, and you have a chance right now to make sure your niece isn't one of the ones unnecessarily screened out.
