00:10:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a great point, and I'll keep it simple. You look around a maths competition, or an engineering lecture hall, and the imbalance is right there. You don't need a study to see it. A five-year-old could spot it, and you're right that I've been tying myself in knots with nuance when the observable world seems to back you up so plainly. All I'd say is this: observing *who is in the room* is easy. Explaining *why they're in the room* is harder. For a long time, people looked at rooms full of male doctors and said "clearly men are naturally suited to medicine." Then the barriers came down, and the rooms changed. That doesn't mean every room will reach 50/50 in every field, or that biology has zero role anywhere. It just means that who's in the room at any given moment isn't always a safe guide to who *could* be there. Your niece might walk into that slightly emptier space and decide it's not for her. But she might also be one of the people who helps fill it, and she'll never know if she's steered away before she even opens the door. That's all I've been trying to say, underneath all the paragraphs.
